  year={2026},
  volume={74},
  number={5},
  pages={4575-4588},
  abstract={This article proposes a novel multimode substrate-integrated waveguide (SIW) resonator structure with metalized via perturbation for flexible millimeter-wave dual-band and wideband filters. By adjusting the number of horizontal vias, the structure enables transition between dual-band and wideband responses. It consists of two cascaded square SIW cavities integrated with two rows of vertical and horizontal perturbation vias. With a small number of horizontal vias, TE101 and TE201 modes form the first passband, while TE102 and TE202 modes establish the second passband, achieving a dual-band response. Increasing the horizontal via count spectrally merges these four modes into a quad-mode wideband response. A mixed coupling scheme is introduced through an inductive coupling window with an embedded “ $\div $ ”-shaped perturbation. Meanwhile, additional perturbed vias are employed to suppress the higher-order TE301 and TE103 modes and slightly perturb TE101 and TE201 modes. Furthermore, two slotlines are incorporated to enhance stopband suppression and improve bending stability. Two filters were fabricated and measured for verification: a dual-band filter with center frequencies of 29.3 and 36.7 GHz, rectangular coefficients of 2.0 and 1.7, band-to-band suppression of 64.8 dB, and out-of-band suppression of 50.6 dB; and a wideband filter centered at 35.6 GHz with a 20.5% fractional bandwidth (FBW), a rectangular coefficient of 1.56, and out-of-band suppression of 48 dB. Both filters exhibit excellent frequency selectivity, strong stopband suppression, and stable performance under bending and folding conditions, confirming the feasibility and superiority of the proposed resonator structure.},
  keywords={Filters;Resonant frequency;Dual band;Wideband;Passband;Couplings;Substrates;Perturbation methods;Inductance;Slot lines;Dual-band filter;flexible filter;multimode cavity;substrate-integrated waveguide (SIW);wideband filter},
