  abstract={This study proposes an accurate parameterization method to address the complexity of physical modeling and numerical simulation of involute gears in high-voltage switch transmission systems, as well as the tedious and difficult process of structured hexahedral mesh partitioning. This method is based on the theory of involute and the main parameters of the gear transmission system, accurately constructing the coordinates of key interpolation points and the shape of the tooth profile. This study adopts a mesh partitioning and symmetry merging generation technique for involute cylindrical spur gear tooth profiles and three-dimensional solid structures. Using C++, the interface is visualized and a human-computer interaction module is established to achieve high-quality and accurate mesh partitioning parameterization. Through in-depth comparative research on contact analysis examples, guided by Hertz contact theory, this study comprehensively compares and verifies Workbench software with a self-developed parametric software platform. The results demonstrate excellent consistency and matching, thus proving the physical modeling and numerical simulation parameterization method provided in this paper. This platform provides an efficient and reliable parametric solution for the physical modeling and numerical simulation of high-voltage switch gear transmission systems, with significant features such as fast modeling capability, accurate structured grid generation technology, and intuitive and easy-to-use user interface.},
